WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:04.200
(gentle spirited music)

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.300
- Long continual learning

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.380
is one of the core interest topics for me,

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.630
and it does relate to both
artificial intelligence

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.575
and neuroscience as Alyssa mentioned.

00:00:15.575 --> 00:00:16.557
So in a sense,

00:00:16.557 --> 00:00:21.050
it's kind of the ultimate
challenge, seems to be these days,

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.410
for artificial intelligence systems

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.480
to kind of bridge the gap between

00:00:26.441 --> 00:00:29.320
the artificial and natural intelligence.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.750
So I will briefly go over
some motivation and history

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.750
of the field, which dates
back to 1950s, if not earlier,

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:41.990
as it started in
computational neuroscience,

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:43.810
and inspirations from neuroscience

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.340
kind of fueled the research for a while.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.210
And most recently there was an explosion

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.450
of different approaches and
settings trying to solve

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.880
their lifelong continual
learning problems,

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.411
especially in deep neural networks.

00:00:58.411 --> 00:01:02.570
I'll also touch upon the
recent most challenging

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.963
and exciting area of continual
reinforcement learning.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.140
And we'll conclude this tutorial

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.260
with some recent open questions

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.220
and perhaps some
controversial discussions,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.180
whether scaling of neural net models

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.420
can solve their main issue
of catastrophic forgetting

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.163
or maybe something more is needed.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.970
So starting with motivation and history,

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:31.970
well, clearly you're all are
aware of impressive results

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.310
achieved by artificial
intelligence systems

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.730
in the past decade from playing games,

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:42.360
and beating human champions,
and chess and poker

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.830
and stone supports to generating paintings

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.210
that actually were successfully sold

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:54.080
at New York art auctions,
using GANs for example,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.960
and definitely achieving

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.150
or sometimes beating
a human level accuracy

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.740
in detecting abnormalities
in medical imaging,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.740
and to the recent year kind
of really amazing results

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.860
in terms of generating text,

00:02:10.803 --> 00:02:13.110
which is pretty much indistinguishable

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:18.110
from human written texts
by the GPT-3 system.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.270
And we're all kind of waiting to see

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.280
when GPT-4 going to be announced

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.670
and what is going to be able to do

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.670
by this idea of scaling
modern neural networks,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.330
and training them also on
a humongous amount of data

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:37.480
seem to be bearing fruit
and we see the results

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.870
that we were never able to see so far.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:44.870
But what is recognized still
as one of the major limitations

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:51.260
is a lack of breadth or
versatility in these AI systems,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.420
because they're still
somewhat narrow specialists.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.080
And as you all know,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.750
we humans and ideally artificial
systems we want to build

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.530
should be able to be
versatile, multitasking,

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.610
and be able to continually
kind of acquire new skills,

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:13.455
and it doesn't necessary
have to be a system

00:03:13.455 --> 00:03:16.070
that is best at everything
and beating human champions

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.640
in all the games,

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.660
but rather we would like to have a system

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.550
that may be good enough,

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.430
but at extremely broad range of tasks,

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.650
just like we humans are.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:27.740
How do we get there?

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.720
How do we achieve this versatility?

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.639
So the are typical paths,
there is through lifelong,

00:03:34.639 --> 00:03:36.210
or continual learning,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.160
essentially constant expansion

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.470
and development of your abilities

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.340
and acquiring increasingly
more complex behaviors

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.740
building upon what you
learned in the past.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.930
And as I will be talking soon,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.613
also avoiding issue called
catastrophic forgetting.

00:03:58.003 --> 00:04:00.610
So examples of practical applications

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:04.580
where artificial systems
will need such versatility

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.760
and ability to learn continually

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.460
range from classical continual learning

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.120
challenge of robotics,

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.360
where the robot has to
learn to do new tasks

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.300
in new environments and
keep kind of learning

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.713
without completely forgetting
what the current day go,

00:04:24.723 --> 00:04:28.900
to obviously at systems that
control autonomous vehicles,

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.500
and change environment

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.923
from country road to the city to highway.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.550
Obviously, systems that maintain
conversations with people,

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.970
conversational agents of different kind,

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:47.010
they should be very flexible
because the situations change

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.420
in which they supposed
to be helping the person,

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.633
and also the person's
mood may be changing.

00:04:53.106 --> 00:04:54.600
And even with one person,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.450
there will be a lot of
adaptations required

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.280
for just that specific person,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.990
leave alone adaptation
of conversational agents

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.603
across different users.

00:05:04.592 --> 00:05:07.580
And here is one of my favorite movies,

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.557
movie "her" where the
conversational agent turned out

00:05:11.557 --> 00:05:14.057
to be extremely flexible
and continually learning.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.177
And examples go on and on,

00:05:18.177 --> 00:05:21.040
there are many, of course,
important medical applications

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.030
such as medical imaging,

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:26.530
where it's really
important to have systems

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:31.530
that can learn on data from
one hospital for example,

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:34.430
and utilize that data
for different hospitals,

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.660
learn continually from patient to patient,

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.110
and from disorder to disorder, because,

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:41.770
unfortunately most of the
time in practice right now,

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.750
you have to retrain those
systems from scratch

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.780
on the new data sets.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.490
But if you believe that

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:51.280
there is something common and
variant about the disorder,

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.687
you really would like to
exploited all the data sets that

00:05:55.687 --> 00:05:58.587
you will be encountering during
the lifetime of the system

00:05:59.488 --> 00:06:02.023
and incorporate that
information in the flexible way.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.740
So what is one of the
key problems identified

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:08.493
with continual learning,

00:06:08.493 --> 00:06:10.473
and this, as I said,

00:06:10.473 --> 00:06:13.180
dates back to the previous
century of course,

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:18.050
way before more than a large
scale deep neural networks,

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:22.010
the problem was encountered multiple times

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.240
in computational neuroscience.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.640
And one most notable paper
was a paper from 1989

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.842
by McCloskey and Cohem,

00:06:30.842 --> 00:06:35.240
where they demonstrated that
when they train neural network

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.340
using backpropagation,

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.180
just the classical standard neural map

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.180
on the problem of learning
how to do one sums ones,

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.590
additional facts, like a
one plus one, two plus one,

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:49.850
three plus one, and so on.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:51.520
The system can learn it well,

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.280
but the moment they switch
to the two addition facts,

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.080
it starts forgetting really quickly

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:57.710
what they've learned before

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:00.530
to the point that in a few trials,

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.000
performance drops to
like 20% from hundred.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.403
And a few more trials,

00:07:05.244 --> 00:07:07.160
and system completely
catastrophically forgets

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.483
what it learned before.

00:07:09.775 --> 00:07:14.775
So this phenomenon actually
is a part of more general

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:18.440
challenge in both
computational neuroscience,

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:23.440
trying to understand how our
brains adapt to new situations,

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:25.690
how plasticity happens

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.540
without losing stability or memory,

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.870
and how do we implement that potentially

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.920
in artificial neural networks.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:36.230
So catastrophic interference
or catastrophic forgetting

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:40.200
is just one part of this general issue,

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.660
because you need both
plasticity to change quickly

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:46.790
when the environment changes,

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:49.020
but you also need stability,

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.220
so you don't forget
what you learned before.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:56.240
And there is a long history
of continual learning

00:07:57.351 --> 00:08:01.420
and this chart is highly
sketchy and incomplete,

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.150
so I apologize in advance,

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.490
but I tried to put
together at least partially

00:08:09.076 --> 00:08:12.240
some points in the history
when terminology was introduced

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.830
with particular important
findings were discovered

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:18.875
and it all can be even dated back

00:08:18.875 --> 00:08:21.090
to the sensitivity stability trade-off

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.970
described by Hebb in 1949,

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:27.530
to the stability plasticity
dilemma that I mentioned

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:28.763
on the previous slide.

00:08:30.181 --> 00:08:33.470
And then our various methods
that we're trying to avoid

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:36.390
catastrophic forgetting mainly
developed during nineties

00:08:37.888 --> 00:08:39.720
in computational neuroscience literature.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.260
And then most recently

00:08:42.853 --> 00:08:47.343
they really were picked up on
in the deep learning community

00:08:48.645 --> 00:08:52.453
because once we managed to
achieve those impressive results

00:08:52.453 --> 00:08:54.060
with deep neural nets in specific tasks,

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.479
the next question was exactly,

00:08:56.479 --> 00:09:00.100
"So now how do we learn this
ability to perform at all

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.500
and be versatile just
like we humans do it."

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:07.230
So recent five years, I would say,

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:09.840
witnessed exponential growth
in the number of papers

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.433
in the continual lifelong learning area.

00:09:15.482 --> 00:09:16.315
Talking about neuroscience,

00:09:16.315 --> 00:09:19.040
there are, of course,
multiple inspirations that

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.960
were used over the past few decades,

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.570
but the most notable ones, I would say,

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:28.300
were related to a frequency of thoughts

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:31.253
that I will describe in
the following three slides.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:38.420
Basically plasticity is, most of the time,

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:40.220
meant to be synaptic.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:42.900
So when we talk about
plasticity of neural net,

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:44.903
we meant adaptation of synaptic Bates,

00:09:46.072 --> 00:09:49.280
and the interesting thing is
that some recent experiments

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:53.000
also kind of support
that earlier hypothesis,

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:57.310
that when we learn a new task,

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:59.180
and in this case it was an experiment

00:10:03.875 --> 00:10:06.400
basically looking at the
strings of the synopsis,

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.420
looking at the size of our
dendritic spines corresponding

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.200
to those when we learn a new task,

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:14.380
some of them get enlarged,

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.750
so they correspond to the synapsis

00:10:18.513 --> 00:10:20.720
that are kind of taking
care of solving the task,

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:21.570
roughly speaking.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:24.810
The interesting thing
is that those changes

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.023
do persist over like
long time like months.

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:31.243
And we do not forget the tasks we learn,

00:10:32.099 --> 00:10:35.090
but if you actually directly intervene

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:38.570
and use some approaches
from synaptic optogenetics,

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.450
you kind of revert those changes,

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:44.086
then the task will be forgotten.

00:10:44.086 --> 00:10:48.587
So it's really telling you
that certain synapsis will be

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:54.990
responsible for representing
and solving particular tasks,

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.360
and therefore you may want
to do something like that,

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.800
when you train your deep neuro lab.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.680
You want to dedicate certain weights,

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:07.110
certain connections to particular tasks,

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:12.030
and make sure that you protect
them from being washed out

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.710
by more recent training.

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:17.300
And this idea gave rise to
multiple approaches in the past,

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.900
including a relatively recent

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.700
elastic weight consolidation method

00:11:23.336 --> 00:11:25.390
that became quite popular
and kind of gave rise

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:27.853
to many recent approaches,

00:11:29.532 --> 00:11:31.832
I'll talk briefly about
that in a few minutes.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:35.410
But I also want to mention another

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.640
kind of quite famous theory

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:42.640
that gave rise to different
computational approaches,

00:11:44.142 --> 00:11:46.863
this is called Complementary
Learning Systems theory.

00:11:48.809 --> 00:11:52.720
So it also dates back to
'90s to 1995, if not earlier,

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:56.663
and the idea is that we have, kind of,

00:11:58.414 --> 00:12:00.693
at least two parts of the memory system,

00:12:00.693 --> 00:12:03.840
we have the fast learning hippocampus,

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.720
that essentially learns
episodic information.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:10.240
And then later on it is
consolidated in neocortex,

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.170
usually during sleep or arrest

00:12:13.417 --> 00:12:15.220
into more structured knowledge

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:18.020
that can be used to generalize

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.217
to all kind of new situations.

00:12:20.217 --> 00:12:23.510
And this inspired whole
bunch of approaches

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:26.430
in the recent continual
learning literature

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.430
that are based on the replay or rehearsal

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:34.340
by analogy with how the
information from hippocampus

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:37.370
is being consolidated during sleep,

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:40.800
and stored in neocortex
by means of replaying

00:12:41.641 --> 00:12:43.080
the activations in hippocampus that

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.223
were associated with
this particular episode.

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:51.810
So essentially this replay
rehearsal approaches

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:55.460
are quite often used in modern methods

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:58.190
for continual learning

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:02.030
and can be also combined with
various other approaches,

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:04.063
I will give some taxonomy of those.

00:13:05.396 --> 00:13:06.229
So finally,

00:13:06.229 --> 00:13:11.120
another kind of notable thitd
inspiration from neuroscience

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:14.780
is the one that led, I guess,

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:17.803
to the modern so-called
architectural methods.

00:13:19.269 --> 00:13:21.030
This inspiration essentially tells you

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:24.580
that sometimes synaptic
plasticity might not be enough.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.580
And sometimes if the environment changes

00:13:28.009 --> 00:13:30.510
drastically and a significant
it might be useful

00:13:31.488 --> 00:13:33.440
to expand the architecture of your network

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.440
by analogy with so-called neurogenesis,

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:40.033
which even happens in
adult mammals and humans,

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.840
particularly adult neurogenesis means

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:48.550
growth and incorporation of
new neurons in specific areas

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:50.500
of the brain, particularly hippocampus.

00:13:52.083 --> 00:13:54.693
It's pretty much the only area
where it happens in humans,

00:13:55.921 --> 00:13:56.754
and other mammals,

00:13:56.754 --> 00:13:58.117
It also may happen in our factory default.

00:13:58.117 --> 00:14:00.890
But the bottom line is
that when they are changes

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:05.010
in the environment are
sufficiently significant,

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:09.240
it may pay off to expand your
architecture of your network

00:14:10.821 --> 00:14:11.871
and loosely speaking,

00:14:12.709 --> 00:14:14.900
that's kind of inspiration to third branch

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:18.980
of modern computational
continual learning methods

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.480
I will be talking about.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.370
So the first one, as I mentioned,

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:27.090
based on the synaptic
plasticity and freezing weights

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:29.140
that are responsible for tasks,

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:32.020
lead to various regularization approaches.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:34.450
The second based on the

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:37.800
separation between slow and fast learning,

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:42.580
hippocampus and neocortex learn to replay,

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:44.660
and then neurogenetic approach has led

00:14:45.507 --> 00:14:48.107
to architecture based methods
in continual learning.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:50.633
Okay.

00:14:50.633 --> 00:14:55.140
So let us know go to the brief overview

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:58.033
of the modern supervised
continual learning,

00:14:58.941 --> 00:15:00.270
because just like there's
machine learning in general,

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:03.810
the supervised part is
most developed these days,

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.540
although there is lots of recent research

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.200
in unsupervised continual learning.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.790
And as I will mention

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:14.640
in the most challenging
domain out of those

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.740
in continual reinforcement learning.

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:21.330
So here is essentially overview

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:25.663
of the continual learning
setting in supervised problems.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:30.050
And it's compared to the
Standard Supervised Learning,

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:32.580
in the standard one, as you know,

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:37.580
you take inputs X and the
label Y in the given data set,

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.760
you assume that there is one distribution

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:46.750
from which the data will be
coming during training and test.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:49.550
You learn the model and
during the test time,

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:52.763
you will be tested on samples
from the same distribution.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.023
However, in continual learning,

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.620
you have a stream

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:02.060
of potentially different
data distributions

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:03.630
and different tasks,

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:08.630
each associated with
its own set of samples.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:12.050
So you have those inputs
and outputs X and Ys

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:14.420
coming from distribution one,

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.170
then switching to distribution two,

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:18.950
and potentially the infinite stream

00:16:20.133 --> 00:16:22.340
of different data distributions.

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:26.733
Your learner acts in online
versus offline manner.

00:16:28.209 --> 00:16:31.610
And it has to essentially
learn all those tasks

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:34.190
because each time it may be tested

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:37.456
on all the previous tasks seen so far,

00:16:37.456 --> 00:16:39.190
that's why you would
like the method to adapt

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:42.990
to new data sets and still be
able to remember how to solve

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:44.163
the previous ones.

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:51.450
Clearly continual learning
relates to many sub areas

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:53.523
of modern machine learning,

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:57.600
that have in common the same component

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:59.970
of having multiple data distributions

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:04.360
and trying to somehow come up with a model

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:08.750
that can make use of all those data,

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:13.623
and also be able to handle at
least some new distributions.

00:17:16.095 --> 00:17:17.470
But multi-task Learning is perhaps

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.310
the most classical
baseline kind of comparison

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:22.880
when you given set of data distributions,

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:26.800
but simultaneously, so
learning is offline,

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:28.577
unlike continual learning .

00:17:30.897 --> 00:17:33.103
And essentially you can shuffle the data,

00:17:34.163 --> 00:17:36.843
which is not an option in
continual online learning.

00:17:39.537 --> 00:17:42.520
And you can essentially use one model,

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:45.330
you're trying to train model
with shared set of parameters,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:46.690
that will work well

00:17:48.363 --> 00:17:50.160
on all these different
tasks simultaneously.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:53.880
During the testing you
will be given samples,

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:56.473
again, coming from
distributions of those tasks,

00:17:57.478 --> 00:17:59.730
but nothing out of distribution.

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:02.433
So there is no continual
model on adaptation here,

00:18:03.322 --> 00:18:04.483
it's offline approach.

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:08.850
Okay. So let me...

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:13.390
So transfer learning is another
kind of closely related,

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:18.161
but simpler setting where you
typically have source tasks

00:18:18.161 --> 00:18:20.653
and target task to data distributions.

00:18:21.519 --> 00:18:23.950
And you try to transfer
knowledge from the model learned

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:28.277
on the first task to perform
better on the second task,

00:18:28.277 --> 00:18:33.277
but you don't really care
about keeping the performance

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:34.333
on the first task.

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:38.370
Again, there is no
continuous adaptation here,

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.143
after you learned the target task,

00:18:41.195 --> 00:18:44.503
there is no online infinite
stream of changing environments.

00:18:45.609 --> 00:18:47.315
And as I mentioned,

00:18:47.315 --> 00:18:49.751
the source task performance
doesn't matter anymore,

00:18:49.751 --> 00:18:52.551
once you transfer you kind
of knowledge to that new one.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:56.440
Domain adaption is also
closely related of course,

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:00.170
so it's kind of a particular
case of transfer learning

00:19:01.142 --> 00:19:03.307
where the source and
target task are the same,

00:19:03.307 --> 00:19:05.107
but the input domains are different.

00:19:06.341 --> 00:19:09.870
Again, same comparison
with continual learning,

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:11.203
this is unidirectional,

00:19:12.657 --> 00:19:16.330
you do not accumulate knowledge
in the infinite stream,

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:19.090
and you do not really
care about performance

00:19:20.429 --> 00:19:22.220
on the previous data sets,

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:24.923
after you transfer
knowledge to the new ones.

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:31.970
Finally, are kind of most popular recently

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:36.690
and kind of rich area of
this type of multitask

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.717
and multi environment
learning is meta-learning.

00:19:39.717 --> 00:19:42.490
And there are many
methods developed recently

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.136
to solve these type of problems,

00:19:44.136 --> 00:19:47.100
but the setting is, again, offline.

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:50.670
You assume that there is
a certain distribution

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:53.480
of distributions of the tasks.

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:57.350
You draw a set of different data sets

00:19:57.350 --> 00:19:59.160
from this distribution.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:04.160
Each data set belongs to
its own data distribution,

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:07.500
and you must train single model,

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:10.920
so that during the test time,

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.377
when it will be presented with yet another

00:20:14.305 --> 00:20:15.620
new data distribution,

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.420
not something you've seen
on the training time,

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:22.503
it will be able to do well on it.

00:20:22.503 --> 00:20:26.270
It will be allowed to adopt
in a few short kind of manner.

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:30.590
So you're really trying to learn a model

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:34.130
that can be easily
adopted to a large variety

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:37.120
of different new datasets,

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:38.620
of course there's certain assumptions

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:41.030
of how they relate to the old data sets

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:42.230
on which you're trained.

00:20:43.073 --> 00:20:47.880
Again, the problem here as
compared to continual learning

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:50.640
is that we're learning offline.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:51.630
We have access

00:20:52.521 --> 00:20:55.103
to all these different
data sets simultaneously.

00:20:57.113 --> 00:20:59.663
And once we switched to the test mode,

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:03.320
we do not usually care about
how well they performed

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:05.020
on the previous data sets.

00:21:06.184 --> 00:21:07.017
So continual learning seem to be

00:21:07.017 --> 00:21:09.920
the most challenging
setting out of all of those,

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.340
because it combines

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:14.680
a temporal sequential
nature of learning with

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:16.820
the desire to transfer knowledge,

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:19.780
not only to the future,

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.663
but also take care of
not forgetting the past.

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:24.353
And by the way,

00:21:24.353 --> 00:21:29.160
a notable important example
related to all the settings

00:21:30.087 --> 00:21:33.008
is support out of
distribution generalization,

00:21:33.008 --> 00:21:36.950
and you perhaps can view it
as seeking specific setting

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:39.963
of meta-learning as
zero shot meta-learning.

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.870
And the classical example comes from the

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:46.600
invariant risk minimization
paper by Arjovsky

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:48.850
and his colleagues at Facebook,

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:52.712
which actually inspired the whole area

00:21:52.712 --> 00:21:56.830
of recent robust prediction papers,

00:21:56.830 --> 00:21:59.773
which I unfortunately don't
have time to cover here,

00:22:00.860 --> 00:22:04.724
but I really encourage you
to take a look at them,

00:22:04.724 --> 00:22:06.030
at related work.

00:22:06.030 --> 00:22:09.503
And the classical example
is that most of the

00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:14.550
modern deep network systems will break,

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:18.460
if they will be asked
to perform on the data

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:20.510
that have distribution
sufficiently different

00:22:20.510 --> 00:22:22.370
from the one they were trained on.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:26.020
For example, if they were
trained to classify animals,

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:30.590
and they only see examples
of cows on green pastures,

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:32.280
and then during tests,

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:35.140
they will certainly see cows on the beach.

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:35.973
Most of the times,

00:22:35.973 --> 00:22:40.973
our system performance will
really go down quite a bit,

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:42.163
and there reason is,

00:22:42.163 --> 00:22:44.390
there's also another interesting paper,

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:48.020
kind of summarizing this
tendency of deep nets

00:22:48.020 --> 00:22:51.040
to rely on so-called shortcut features

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:53.340
or spuriously correlated features,

00:22:53.340 --> 00:22:56.350
which do not have really
anything to do with

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:58.310
the concept of animal here,

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:00.700
but they are easier to detect and rely on

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:03.040
such as a green background.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:07.175
And the goal of our recent approach

00:23:07.175 --> 00:23:10.530
is to more and robust out of
distribution generalization,

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:15.530
including that famous paper
by Arjovsky is to figure out,

00:23:17.723 --> 00:23:20.150
how to regularize, how
to constrain your model,

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:21.830
and the process of learning it,

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:24.400
so that you will be able to

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:27.390
extract those invariant features.

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:29.500
For example, of animal or a cow,

00:23:29.500 --> 00:23:32.630
while ignoring the
spurious shortcut features

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:35.402
such as color of the background.

00:23:35.402 --> 00:23:36.560
And there are different
approaches to that, as I said,

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:40.580
it's extremely interesting
and the rapidly growing area,

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:42.681
I encourage you to look into.

00:23:42.681 --> 00:23:44.281
But very briefly the idea of CRM

00:23:45.502 --> 00:23:48.750
is that you will be aiming
at learning and mapping

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:52.850
from the input from the pixel space

00:23:52.850 --> 00:23:55.000
into representation space.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:59.060
So that essentially it will
be able to learn invariant

00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:01.640
or robust set of features there,

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:04.820
so that you can be simultaneously optimal

00:24:05.883 --> 00:24:08.960
with this set of features
in terms of predicting

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:13.960
the type of animal across a
variety of different data sets,

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:15.893
or support environments.

00:24:17.208 --> 00:24:19.271
And that's kind of the way

00:24:19.271 --> 00:24:22.900
the other distribution setting
connects with meta-learning.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:26.030
At the train time, you will be
given a set of environments,

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:28.870
and your job is to find features

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:30.683
that are robust or invariant,

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.767
across all the environments

00:24:33.767 --> 00:24:38.063
with respect to predicting
that last label.

00:24:38.063 --> 00:24:38.896
Okay.

00:24:40.395 --> 00:24:44.667
So back to Continue Learning
Setting, as I mentioned,

00:24:44.667 --> 00:24:48.870
the main challenge that
distinguishes this setting

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:52.023
from all the previous
ones that I mentioned,

00:24:52.948 --> 00:24:55.740
is it happens online and
it happens sequentially,

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:58.260
and unlike out-of-distribution

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:00.740
or meta-learning type of settings,

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:05.510
you do not have access
to all the previous tasks

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:07.310
when you are switching to a new one.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:11.113
So what kind of particular
situations can happen here?

00:25:12.364 --> 00:25:15.343
So assuming you have access
input-X vector, Y-class label,

00:25:16.454 --> 00:25:21.030
and you have the task or
context such as, I don't know,

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:25.530
classifying animals and then
classifying, I don't know,

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:29.770
buildings to classify plants
and so on and so forth.

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:31.220
What can happen here?

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:35.300
You may or may not be told
what task you're doing now.

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:38.523
So the team may be
observed or not observed.

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:41.540
And it also can be observed

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:45.580
both during training and test time or not.

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:49.800
Depending on which of
these conditions satisfied,

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:53.390
you will have several
different type of settings

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:55.570
for our continual learning.

00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:59.950
And briefly speaking, if
you observe the task ID,

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:02.940
if you have Oracle telling
you when task changes

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:05.520
during training, it's a simplest case

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:08.480
called task-incremental
continual learning,

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:10.800
well, if that's not the case,

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:12.354
and when the new task arrive,

00:26:12.354 --> 00:26:16.150
you will kind of potentially
even have new classes

00:26:17.009 --> 00:26:18.850
that will be called
class-incremental continual learning.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:22.760
And there's also related sitting

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:25.010
called domain-incremental
continual learning.

00:26:25.928 --> 00:26:29.790
And when you just not know any
boundary between those tasks,

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:32.633
it's the most challenging
task agnostic settings.

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:37.660
Anyway, I'll probably not going
to have much time to go into

00:26:37.660 --> 00:26:41.670
the details, and just give
you the high level idea.

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:45.223
But, as I said, task-incremental
is the simplest setting,

00:26:46.389 --> 00:26:48.910
when model is always aware
of which task it's solving.

00:26:48.910 --> 00:26:49.743
For example,

00:26:49.743 --> 00:26:52.590
it's told that now you will
be classifying animals.

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:54.890
Now you're classifying buildings.

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:57.580
And in this situation it's simple

00:26:58.571 --> 00:27:00.660
to have some multi-head architecture

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:03.850
and try to load common representation.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:06.973
But for each specific task, you
will have its own predictor.

00:27:08.817 --> 00:27:12.603
There main assumption here is
just that the set of labels

00:27:13.510 --> 00:27:15.640
that you will be predicting in each task

00:27:17.456 --> 00:27:19.600
might change across time.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:21.410
And that's a schematic representation

00:27:21.410 --> 00:27:22.730
of what I was just saying.

00:27:22.730 --> 00:27:26.250
During training, you have
a clear task boundaries.

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:31.010
And during tests, you also
are told what to classify now,

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:33.883
whether it's like animal
tasks or the building tasks,

00:27:35.415 --> 00:27:39.532
and multi-head architecture is a easy way

00:27:39.532 --> 00:27:41.332
to handle this problem, essentially.

00:27:42.435 --> 00:27:44.160
So what isn't common is
the rest of the network,

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:46.640
which learns good representation across

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:47.940
all these different tasks.

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:52.261
The next more challenging setting

00:27:52.261 --> 00:27:57.261
is when you are not given the
task identity during tests,

00:27:59.815 --> 00:28:03.480
and that picture kind of
summarizes what's going on,

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:06.623
during training you know
that you are classifying,

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:12.150
say, images in particular tasks,

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:16.550
say, animals and then buildings
and so on and so forth.

00:28:16.550 --> 00:28:21.550
While during test, you are
not told where that image,

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:25.080
the test image comes
from a particular task.

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:27.380
You need to figure out
which task it came from

00:28:29.117 --> 00:28:31.850
and as well classify properly.

00:28:31.850 --> 00:28:34.679
And finally is there is a related

00:28:34.679 --> 00:28:37.260
domain-incremental continual learning ,

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:40.655
which only difference
from the previous one

00:28:40.655 --> 00:28:41.903
is specific assumption,

00:28:43.414 --> 00:28:46.033
and that assumption is that distribution,

00:28:46.972 --> 00:28:49.310
and distribution of class
labels does not change.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:51.520
Just to give you an
example of what it means

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:55.610
in a specific data set.

00:28:55.610 --> 00:29:00.610
Imagine you are given the
task of learning a sequence

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:02.500
of split MNIST task.

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:06.543
So first you have to learn
to classify zero from one,

00:29:07.871 --> 00:29:10.200
then two from three,
and so on and so forth.

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:12.035
So that's why it's called split MNIST,

00:29:12.035 --> 00:29:15.293
because it's splitted into
several separate tasks.

00:29:16.688 --> 00:29:19.540
And here is what happens in
each of the three scenarios

00:29:19.540 --> 00:29:20.890
that I mentioned.

00:29:20.890 --> 00:29:23.083
So with incremental-task learning,

00:29:24.560 --> 00:29:25.727
you will be given the task,

00:29:25.727 --> 00:29:28.670
you will be told that you
solving task 1 or task 3

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:32.230
or task 5, both during
training and during tests.

00:29:32.230 --> 00:29:35.040
And your only question is to figure out,

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:36.580
to answer correctly the question,

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:40.290
whether the image belongs to
the first class or the second,

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:42.070
is it zero or one, or, eight or nine,

00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:43.770
but you know the identity of task.

00:29:44.713 --> 00:29:49.713
And incremental-domain
learning task is not known,

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:55.310
but you just want to know
whether your class label

00:29:55.310 --> 00:29:58.120
belongs to the first or second class,

00:29:58.120 --> 00:29:59.520
you need to figure that.

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:02.393
In incremental-class learning,
you also need to figure,

00:30:03.553 --> 00:30:05.860
not just whether it's
first or second level,

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:07.840
but which digit it is.

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:10.940
So you also need to figure out which task

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:12.180
that sample came from.

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:16.230
So that's kind of most challenging
task-incremental setting.

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:19.970
You will have to keep
learning all those objects

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:20.950
of different classes,

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:23.233
and the number of pluses keeps increasing.

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:26.000
And finally, as I mentioned,

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:28.710
even more challenging
setting is when the identity

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:31.600
of the task is not given to you,

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:36.270
neither during tests nor during training.

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:39.170
And essentially you may
need to be able to detect

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:43.130
kind of nonstationarity,

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:45.580
detect the change between
the tasks in your data.

00:30:48.673 --> 00:30:53.459
There are multiple other
kind of frameworks that were,

00:30:53.459 --> 00:30:55.730
and the fittings that kind
of were developed recently,

00:30:55.730 --> 00:30:57.450
including method,

00:30:57.450 --> 00:31:00.560
meta-continual earning and
continual-meta learning

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:03.953
kind of schematically represented
here, so on and so forth.

00:31:04.994 --> 00:31:07.844
And in general the field of
continual learning seem to be

00:31:09.456 --> 00:31:13.620
having the explosion of
specific problem settings.

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:16.593
And that's kind of one of the issues,

00:31:18.303 --> 00:31:21.920
how to kind of organize them
in some unifying framework.

00:31:21.920 --> 00:31:25.400
So, you essentially, I can compare methods

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:27.500
like you can compare apples to oranges.

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:30.650
So you know what exactly your
setting the assumes about

00:31:32.941 --> 00:31:35.090
the changes in the environment,
changes in the tasks,

00:31:35.090 --> 00:31:37.830
what information is
available to your learner,

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:40.280
and what's the evaluation criteria are?

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:42.180
So there is lots of work in this area,

00:31:43.238 --> 00:31:45.970
one particular paper
you could take a look at

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:51.040
from last year and Marius
was trying to unify

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:52.370
these previous settings,

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:55.900
including meta-continual
and continual-meta

00:31:55.900 --> 00:32:00.530
in one framework and essentially expand

00:32:01.409 --> 00:32:02.309
the meta-learning,

00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:06.523
metal learning algorithm,
the famous MAML model,

00:32:07.755 --> 00:32:08.650
agnostic meta-learning
to the continual setting

00:32:08.650 --> 00:32:11.090
with quite a successful results.

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:15.080
Showing that in that
kind of a general setting

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:16.423
using this approach,

00:32:17.575 --> 00:32:20.320
indeed pays off and beats a state over.

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:23.790
Again, I'm unable to go
into all the details,

00:32:23.790 --> 00:32:26.173
and encourage you to
look them at this paper.

00:32:29.188 --> 00:32:32.043
And once they're back,

00:32:33.378 --> 00:32:36.506
just to see what exactly the challenge is,

00:32:36.506 --> 00:32:40.390
so we can go to the methods
and see how different classes

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:44.950
or types of approaches try
to attack that challenge.

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:48.860
So essentially what you're
trying to do in this online,

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:51.470
non-stationary continue learning.

00:32:51.470 --> 00:32:55.740
As I mentioned, you have
sequence of data distributions

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:58.670
from which data samples were drawn.

00:32:58.670 --> 00:33:02.523
Ideally, if you had all
this data available to you,

00:33:03.672 --> 00:33:06.380
or you just kept storing
the infinite stream of data,

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:09.100
your natural objective would be

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:13.253
your empirical risk minimization
across all this tasks.

00:33:14.467 --> 00:33:16.780
You would have summed the expected risks

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:19.130
for each task across all of them.

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:23.820
Unfortunately, you do not
have access to previous tasks,

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:27.020
either you do a real time
nature of the problem

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:28.650
like in robot learning,

00:33:28.650 --> 00:33:32.570
or simply due to the
issue of your inability

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:36.063
to, maybe, just store
infinite amounts of data.

00:33:37.430 --> 00:33:39.320
If you intention is to build a system

00:33:39.320 --> 00:33:42.900
that can learn lifelong infinitely,

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:47.900
plus sometimes considerations
are basically cost efficiency.

00:33:48.150 --> 00:33:52.530
If you running continual
learning agents on edge devices,

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:53.710
on your phone

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:57.530
or on the robot with some
limited memory on it.

00:33:57.530 --> 00:34:02.350
It's simply infeasible to
keep all the data accumulated

00:34:02.350 --> 00:34:04.010
during the robot lifetime.

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:07.430
And constant communication
with some centralized server

00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:10.033
may be also expensive
or not always possible.

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:15.890
So what happens in this
situation, as I mentioned,

00:34:15.890 --> 00:34:19.260
the catastrophic forgetting
problem in general

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:20.340
that was observed

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:23.020
in the computational
neuroscience literature,

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:24.250
keeps popping up here.

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:27.740
And the classical example
from the EWC paper

00:34:30.450 --> 00:34:33.890
showed that standard
stochastic gradient descent

00:34:33.890 --> 00:34:36.200
apply to a sequence of tasks,

00:34:36.200 --> 00:34:39.500
will train and do well in the first task,

00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:42.793
but the moment it starts
adopting to second task,

00:34:44.696 --> 00:34:47.460
information about the previous
task is being washed out,

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:49.050
performance degrades.

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:51.610
The new task is shown
performance keeps degrading

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:52.920
further and further.

00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:55.290
Therefore you need to do something,

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:58.820
some form of memory to avoid kind of this

00:34:58.820 --> 00:35:00.420
catastrophic forgetting issue

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:03.720
of a naive standard neural net learning

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:04.970
with stochastic gradient.

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:09.980
And essentially this is, as I mentioned,

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:14.370
more general problem of
stability versus plasticity,

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:18.298
which in a sense that I can be viewed

00:35:18.298 --> 00:35:21.710
as a program of knowledge
transfer to the future

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:25.370
to perform hopefully better after learning

00:35:25.370 --> 00:35:29.600
from several tasks and
lack of interference,

00:35:29.600 --> 00:35:32.833
or also kind of positive
transfer to the past,

00:35:33.767 --> 00:35:36.650
at least not forgetting how
to do well on previous data

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:38.963
and perhaps even improve your performance.

00:35:40.606 --> 00:35:42.220
So that's the ultimate goal.

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:46.696
One common way of measuring
transfer and interference

00:35:46.696 --> 00:35:49.720
in various approaches, I'll
mention some of them briefly,

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:54.720
is to simply look at the
dot product of gradients

00:35:57.829 --> 00:36:00.470
at their samples from new
tasks and the previous tasks.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:03.053
And essentially you can
think about that as,

00:36:04.419 --> 00:36:08.160
if my current step in
terms of updating based

00:36:09.057 --> 00:36:10.143
on my new samples,

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:15.420
goes in the opposite
direction for them over

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:18.740
the direction of the gradient
from the previous task,

00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:20.080
I'm kind of forgetting,

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:23.150
I'm kind of making model perform worse

00:36:24.052 --> 00:36:24.885
on my previous samples.

00:36:24.885 --> 00:36:27.880
So ideally I would like
those gradients to be aligned

00:36:27.880 --> 00:36:30.200
and that would facilitate transfer,

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:33.827
while gradients looking in
the opposite directions,

00:36:33.827 --> 00:36:37.350
are most likely going to lead
to interference between the task

00:36:39.094 --> 00:36:40.863
and that may lead to
catastrophic forgetting.

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:45.570
Just to kind of summarize
possible scenarios

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:49.090
of what can happen with
transfer to the future,

00:36:49.090 --> 00:36:52.690
and transfer for not forgetting the past.

00:36:52.690 --> 00:36:54.870
There are roughly just five situations

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:58.870
which are nicely described
in the recent survey

00:36:58.870 --> 00:37:01.610
from kind of last year
on continual learning,

00:37:01.610 --> 00:37:03.560
reference below.

00:37:03.560 --> 00:37:06.083
And there are some stations
that are desirable,

00:37:06.927 --> 00:37:08.927
and others that you would like to avoid.

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:13.970
So too bad situations are forgetting,

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:15.420
as we already mentioned,

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:19.653
when performance on the
earlier tasks keeps degrading.

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:24.630
But another also bad situation
is when you're too stable

00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:26.320
and you lack plasticity.

00:37:26.320 --> 00:37:29.370
So you learn the first task
and perform well on that,

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:31.653
but you are unable to learn anything new.

00:37:33.790 --> 00:37:34.880
On the contrary,

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:38.310
good situations would
be when either you have

00:37:38.310 --> 00:37:41.520
both stability plasticity
and you keep performing

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:43.930
as well as you did on previous task

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:46.810
while learning new tests.

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:50.826
Possibly, learning
previous tasks helps you

00:37:50.826 --> 00:37:53.590
to actually improve
learning of the new task.

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:56.570
It's even positive, forward transfer,

00:37:56.570 --> 00:38:00.470
and the ideal situation is
not just positive forward,

00:38:00.470 --> 00:38:03.410
but also positive backward transfer,

00:38:03.410 --> 00:38:07.430
when learning new tasks helps
you to even perform better

00:38:07.430 --> 00:38:08.463
on the previous one.

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:11.857
So that's what can happen.

00:38:13.041 --> 00:38:16.057
What really happens in
practice mainly depends on

00:38:17.447 --> 00:38:21.600
both data properties
and method properties.

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:23.240
And right now,

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:25.860
I just want to say that the
field of continual learning

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:28.140
is quite empirical.

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:31.530
There is a proliferation
of different approaches,

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:35.330
and there is a clear lack
of theoretical understanding

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:38.620
of what works when, and where?

00:38:38.620 --> 00:38:41.840
So just to give you some
high level overview,

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:43.073
I want to mention,

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:45.650
as I kind of alluded to it before,

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:50.120
talking about neuroscientific
inspirations replay methods,

00:38:50.120 --> 00:38:54.970
as you remember this support
memory-based replay methods.

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:57.950
I assume that you will replay

00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:00.280
or somehow keep some
representative samples

00:39:00.280 --> 00:39:01.970
of previous tasks,

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:06.970
and that will help you
to not completely forget

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:10.400
the information about the previous tasks

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:11.750
while adapting to new ones.

00:39:14.831 --> 00:39:18.047
So another approach is not
to keep any kind of samples

00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:20.453
in memory,

00:39:21.456 --> 00:39:23.710
but really trying to operate
on the weights that you learn.

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:25.210
And that's the second category

00:39:26.147 --> 00:39:27.910
of support regularization based methods.

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:30.823
As I mentioned before, in
your science inspirations,

00:39:32.346 --> 00:39:36.000
they relate to this idea that
when we learn a certain tasks,

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:37.020
particular weights,

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:41.453
particular synapsis are
assigned to those tasks.

00:39:42.371 --> 00:39:43.960
And we may want to protect those weights

00:39:43.960 --> 00:39:47.330
from future changes,
when learning new tasks.

00:39:47.330 --> 00:39:50.400
So there is a whole class of
approach based on this idea.

00:39:50.400 --> 00:39:52.931
And finally, again,

00:39:52.931 --> 00:39:56.650
very roughly kind of along the
lines of neurogenetic ideas

00:39:56.650 --> 00:39:59.780
from neuroscience, that are
so-called architecture based

00:40:01.148 --> 00:40:02.440
or parameter isolation methods,

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:05.893
where you are allowed to
expand your architecture,

00:40:06.827 --> 00:40:10.750
if needed, to accommodate the new tasks,

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:13.780
and the need to represent
more information.

00:40:13.780 --> 00:40:16.640
Again, we don't have much time remaining,

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:21.250
and I will only very, very
briefly kind of give you

00:40:21.250 --> 00:40:25.090
the taxonomy of those
methods with a few examples.

00:40:25.090 --> 00:40:29.260
You are very welcome to
read this a third way

00:40:29.260 --> 00:40:31.670
that I'm citing here, right.

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:34.000
The launch at our continual learning,

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:36.710
at the end of this lecture,

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:41.090
I will also give you a list
of other recent tutorials.

00:40:41.090 --> 00:40:41.923
As I mentioned,

00:40:41.923 --> 00:40:45.050
the number of methods proposed
recently is really large,

00:40:45.050 --> 00:40:47.400
and keeps growing very rapidly.

00:40:47.400 --> 00:40:51.593
So replay methods have several subtypes,

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:56.170
kind of based on this
rehearsal of pseudo rehearsals

00:40:57.316 --> 00:41:00.510
or not storing the exact
samples from the past,

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:04.403
but rather generating them
from some generative models

00:41:04.403 --> 00:41:06.233
with a constrained methods.

00:41:07.171 --> 00:41:08.673
I have a few examples,

00:41:08.673 --> 00:41:11.270
and I apologize for really
going extremely quickly,

00:41:11.270 --> 00:41:14.640
our gradient episodic memory paper,

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:18.600
very nice one based on the
constraint that you want

00:41:18.600 --> 00:41:23.190
the gradients of old
and new kind of samples

00:41:24.514 --> 00:41:26.530
to at least not contradict each other.

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:28.823
Therefore the constraint at the bottom,

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:32.400
that product of the gradients
should be non-negative

00:41:34.308 --> 00:41:37.340
at that point a generated
state about results.

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:40.160
Later on, meta-experience replay approach

00:41:40.160 --> 00:41:41.210
in the same category.

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:45.570
You use replay previous
samples to keep information

00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:48.920
from representative information
from previous tasks.

00:41:48.920 --> 00:41:52.130
You use the idea of
gradient alignment as well,

00:41:52.130 --> 00:41:54.690
but here you make it
part of your objective,

00:41:54.690 --> 00:41:58.980
rather than just a constraint
and you actually beat

00:42:00.886 --> 00:42:02.180
the previous method, and some others.

00:42:02.180 --> 00:42:05.470
And again, this is not
the most recent method,

00:42:05.470 --> 00:42:10.140
there are even better ones
right now, so on and so forth.

00:42:10.140 --> 00:42:10.973
Generative replay,

00:42:10.973 --> 00:42:15.310
where instead of storing
samples of previous tasks,

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.370
you rather build generative models,

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:21.370
which can generate representative samples

00:42:21.370 --> 00:42:24.563
and add those to our current data set.

00:42:26.210 --> 00:42:29.630
So basically so-called pseudo rehearsal

00:42:29.630 --> 00:42:31.670
or generative replay type of methods.

00:42:31.670 --> 00:42:34.810
And there are many
approaches along this line.

00:42:34.810 --> 00:42:38.823
Again, I highly recommend this
recent paper by van de Van,

00:42:39.760 --> 00:42:41.223
on this type of approaches.

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:47.077
There is second class, roughly
speaking, besides the replay,

00:42:47.077 --> 00:42:49.210
the regularization based methods.

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:51.390
And there are many of them,

00:42:51.390 --> 00:42:55.610
again, you're welcome to
look at the survey listed

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:56.953
at the bottom of the page.

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:00.182
Classical examples,

00:43:00.182 --> 00:43:04.390
is a famous elastic weight
consolidation paper from 2017,

00:43:04.390 --> 00:43:06.940
which by now is of course out performed

00:43:06.940 --> 00:43:08.340
by many recent approaches,

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:12.590
but it's a classical benchmark
for various comparisons.

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:15.700
And the idea there was precisely motivated

00:43:15.700 --> 00:43:20.060
by the the synaptic
consolidation inspiration

00:43:20.060 --> 00:43:21.150
from neuroscience,

00:43:21.150 --> 00:43:26.150
that you try to protect the weights

00:43:27.538 --> 00:43:30.360
that are most important
for learning previous task

00:43:30.360 --> 00:43:35.360
from changes imposed while
you're adapting to new tasks,

00:43:35.860 --> 00:43:38.990
which way you do it precisely
through regularization

00:43:40.255 --> 00:43:41.088
and details.

00:43:41.088 --> 00:43:42.666
You can see in the paper,

00:43:42.666 --> 00:43:45.040
there is a whole bunch of
approaches along the same lines,

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:48.303
which different terms of
what regularizer you use,

00:43:49.300 --> 00:43:53.143
the idea of protecting the
specific weights is common.

00:43:53.980 --> 00:43:54.863
And again,

00:43:55.973 --> 00:43:58.860
the classical picture that
is often used to demonstrate

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:01.050
how to avoid catastrophic forgetting,

00:44:01.050 --> 00:44:03.113
forgetting is this one.

00:44:05.090 --> 00:44:08.210
Finally the so-called parameter isolation

00:44:08.210 --> 00:44:10.650
or architectural approaches methods

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:13.520
also have several sub classes,

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:16.150
some fixed network architecture,

00:44:16.150 --> 00:44:19.870
but try to pack the
weights for different tasks

00:44:19.870 --> 00:44:21.760
into different parts of the network,

00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:24.283
while others actually
expand the architecture.

00:44:26.003 --> 00:44:29.310
And the list of methods here is quite long

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:32.920
from the relatively
simplistic Packnet paper,

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:36.800
where the network is fixed and
you kind of try to allocate

00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:40.970
sparse subsets of weights
to different tasks.

00:44:40.970 --> 00:44:45.970
And then you keep kind of
learning the remaining ones

00:44:46.240 --> 00:44:47.453
for the new tasks.

00:44:49.081 --> 00:44:50.692
And essentially, yeah,

00:44:50.692 --> 00:44:54.693
this approach has some advantages
over just fix that works.

00:44:57.621 --> 00:45:00.094
And the second type of work was expanding

00:45:00.094 --> 00:45:01.563
architecture's progressive networks,

00:45:02.516 --> 00:45:05.856
but a kind of on the add
fixed number of neurons,

00:45:05.856 --> 00:45:08.590
while in this method of
dynamic expendable networks,

00:45:08.590 --> 00:45:12.200
they were really being smart
about how many new neurons

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:14.080
to add, so on and so forth.

00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:16.663
There are many interesting
papers along those lines.

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:20.610
There are very different
style of approaches,

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:23.620
still falling into the
category of architectural.

00:45:23.620 --> 00:45:24.553
For example,

00:45:25.491 --> 00:45:28.423
I really liked this neural
dirichlet process paper,

00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:33.440
essentially, they use their
kind of Bayesian approach

00:45:33.440 --> 00:45:36.820
to building sub-networks or experts.

00:45:36.820 --> 00:45:39.120
And only introducing a new one,

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:43.730
when the data cannot be explained
well by the previous ones

00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:46.743
and they show how to apply it
in continual learning setting.

00:45:48.370 --> 00:45:51.050
Yet another approach directly motivated

00:45:52.387 --> 00:45:53.250
by neurogenesis ideas,

00:45:53.250 --> 00:45:55.780
I mentioned it with
other paper awhile ago,

00:45:55.780 --> 00:45:57.020
on essentially

00:45:57.020 --> 00:46:02.020
how to expand and compress
if needed hidden layers

00:46:03.910 --> 00:46:07.960
so that you will adapt to
changing sequence of tasks.

00:46:07.960 --> 00:46:09.806
But at the same time,

00:46:09.806 --> 00:46:12.790
you avoid extending the model infinitely,

00:46:12.790 --> 00:46:15.360
an analogy of this human neurogenesis

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:19.680
is that we have the birth of
new neurons in hippocampus,

00:46:19.680 --> 00:46:24.313
but we also have deaths of
neurons that are not being used.

00:46:25.160 --> 00:46:28.500
And essentially if the
complexity of environment

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:30.200
is relatively low,

00:46:30.200 --> 00:46:34.140
there are not much neurogenesis
on you neurons is remaining,

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:36.340
essentially ,it's kind
of use it or lose it,

00:46:37.538 --> 00:46:38.570
type of a situation.

00:46:38.570 --> 00:46:39.403
And indeed,

00:46:39.403 --> 00:46:43.370
if you show a sufficiently
rich and changing

00:46:43.370 --> 00:46:45.910
set of environments to your model

00:46:45.910 --> 00:46:50.400
or an empirical neuroscientific
papers to your animal

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:55.400
like I put brat into a reach
and changing environment,

00:46:55.500 --> 00:47:00.200
more neurogenesis happens
and also performance on the

00:47:00.200 --> 00:47:02.890
kind of continual learning
type of tasks is better.

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:06.420
So that's what we also
observed with our kind of our

00:47:06.420 --> 00:47:08.103
simple autoencoder model,

00:47:09.834 --> 00:47:14.010
which in the neurogenetic
setting was ending up with models

00:47:14.010 --> 00:47:17.615
that were always adapting
better to new data

00:47:17.615 --> 00:47:19.320
and not forgetting old data.

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:24.320
While same size but fixed size
models were not as flexible,

00:47:24.331 --> 00:47:27.550
as were kind of found empirically,

00:47:27.550 --> 00:47:30.840
essentially supporting
the empirical observations

00:47:30.840 --> 00:47:34.453
from those neuroscience
papers on neurogenesis.

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:39.490
Anyways, so just kind of
to conclude this whole idea

00:47:40.482 --> 00:47:44.923
of expanding networks
and growing architecture

00:47:46.017 --> 00:47:48.069
with some higher level view

00:47:48.069 --> 00:47:50.130
of what the role of
continual learning could be,

00:47:50.130 --> 00:47:52.283
as you might've heard,

00:47:53.140 --> 00:47:56.777
there is lots of discussions
and papers along the lines of

00:47:56.777 --> 00:48:00.920
compositionality and
recombinations of certain

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:06.100
basic independent components or mechanisms

00:48:06.100 --> 00:48:08.830
in order to improve models,

00:48:08.830 --> 00:48:13.460
generalization to say
out-of-distribution type of data

00:48:13.460 --> 00:48:14.293
and so on.

00:48:14.293 --> 00:48:18.750
So the idea here could be
that exposing your model

00:48:20.349 --> 00:48:24.120
to challenging, diverse
and complex environments

00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:25.830
in continual learning setting,

00:48:25.830 --> 00:48:30.830
can be a method for
producing this expanding set

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:35.200
of the core mechanisms
that can be recombined

00:48:36.130 --> 00:48:39.834
to solve any problem that will
come your way in the future.

00:48:39.834 --> 00:48:44.560
If you assume that all those
datasets problems or tasks

00:48:45.763 --> 00:48:48.250
are from the world that
continual learning agents

00:48:48.250 --> 00:48:49.203
should live in.

00:48:50.590 --> 00:48:53.820
If they allow for some final basis,

00:48:53.820 --> 00:48:56.160
because each problem
in supervised learning

00:48:56.160 --> 00:48:58.960
can be seen as a function
approximation problem.

00:48:58.960 --> 00:49:01.970
If this set of functions
can be well approximated

00:49:01.970 --> 00:49:03.320
in some finite basis,

00:49:03.320 --> 00:49:06.063
by certain recombination
of this basis functions,

00:49:07.438 --> 00:49:10.160
then you have a hope of
learning finite network

00:49:10.160 --> 00:49:12.760
that can deal with infinite set of tasks,

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:15.460
as long as they they're
coming from the world with the

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:17.530
properties that I just mentioned.

00:49:17.530 --> 00:49:22.530
Somewhat similar to how we
develop the finite size brain

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:24.700
with certain components responsible

00:49:24.700 --> 00:49:28.020
for different, somewhat, orthogonal tasks,

00:49:28.020 --> 00:49:30.240
motor visual, auditory.

00:49:30.240 --> 00:49:32.500
And recombination of those allows us

00:49:33.429 --> 00:49:36.013
to successfully solve
many different problems.

00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:40.128
I know I'm running a
little bit out of time,

00:49:40.128 --> 00:49:44.003
but I just wanted to very
briefly mentioned this new,

00:49:46.534 --> 00:49:48.630
and most challenging and exciting field

00:49:48.630 --> 00:49:50.783
of continual reinforcement learning,

00:49:52.744 --> 00:49:53.863
which is as I said,

00:49:54.865 --> 00:49:57.110
more challenging than supervised learning,

00:49:57.110 --> 00:50:01.080
because you have agent actively
affecting the environment

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:05.430
in which this agent acts,
without too much detail,

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:09.793
I would refer you to already
recent survey on continual RL.

00:50:12.491 --> 00:50:14.970
And essentially it goes
over different sources

00:50:14.970 --> 00:50:17.870
of non-stationary care and
different types of them,

00:50:17.870 --> 00:50:20.470
whether it depends on
the environment only,

00:50:20.470 --> 00:50:25.470
or environment is also
affected by actions of agent.

00:50:25.770 --> 00:50:29.383
And that was a non-stationarity
or combinations of that,

00:50:30.657 --> 00:50:32.367
it gives taxonomy of existing approaches

00:50:32.367 --> 00:50:35.060
to continual reinforcement learning.

00:50:35.060 --> 00:50:39.670
And it discusses a desirable
properties of benchmarks

00:50:39.670 --> 00:50:44.670
because it seemed to be one of
the key important components

00:50:46.700 --> 00:50:51.373
of making continual reinforcement
learning successful.

00:50:51.373 --> 00:50:56.373
How to design sufficiently
challenging benchmarks.

00:50:56.700 --> 00:51:01.309
Also in some controlled
ways that you can increase

00:51:01.309 --> 00:51:02.270
their complexity and you control that,

00:51:02.270 --> 00:51:03.420
and so on and so forth.

00:51:04.570 --> 00:51:06.280
It also discusses different metrics

00:51:06.280 --> 00:51:09.820
way beyond just cumulative reward

00:51:09.820 --> 00:51:12.823
that you would like
your agent to optimize.

00:51:13.968 --> 00:51:16.980
And that's kind of interesting
food for thought as well,

00:51:16.980 --> 00:51:20.410
when you develop new
methods, so on and so forth.

00:51:20.410 --> 00:51:24.910
It discusses open problems
in particular this area

00:51:26.877 --> 00:51:29.760
and inspirations from neuroscience
that you could borrow,

00:51:29.760 --> 00:51:32.780
just building upon the previous successes

00:51:33.719 --> 00:51:36.850
of neural inspired approaches
and continual learning.

00:51:36.850 --> 00:51:40.177
And the final point that
I just wanted to make,

00:51:40.177 --> 00:51:42.320
oh, two final points,

00:51:42.320 --> 00:51:47.070
very briefly is this is
kind of just advertisement

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:51.600
of recently developed
a unifying framework,

00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:56.590
a library for different
settings methods on

00:51:56.590 --> 00:51:58.380
kind of continual learning problems,

00:51:58.380 --> 00:52:01.840
including supervised and
reinforcement learning

00:52:01.840 --> 00:52:02.673
called Sequoia.

00:52:03.770 --> 00:52:05.910
Yeah, if you're interested I
can always provide you a link

00:52:05.910 --> 00:52:08.810
of GitHub, it's an open source,

00:52:08.810 --> 00:52:11.860
and you're welcome to use
it and build on top of that.

00:52:11.860 --> 00:52:15.290
It was developed by students at Mila,

00:52:15.290 --> 00:52:19.640
Fabrice, Normandin, and Massimo
are large group of people

00:52:19.640 --> 00:52:22.210
working with them during the past year.

00:52:22.210 --> 00:52:24.464
And finally, an open question.

00:52:24.464 --> 00:52:26.870
There is lots of
discussions recently about

00:52:26.870 --> 00:52:31.053
the role of scaling in neural networks,

00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:36.050
especially motivated by
recent successes of GPT-3.

00:52:36.050 --> 00:52:39.500
And there is one of the
recent talks along those lines

00:52:40.463 --> 00:52:42.670
about explaining neural
scaling laws, by the way,

00:52:42.670 --> 00:52:46.070
for those of you who are not
yet familiar with this area,

00:52:46.070 --> 00:52:50.320
the scaling laws are empirical
kind of observations about

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:54.300
how various neural net
architectures behave

00:52:54.300 --> 00:52:56.053
on different types of tasks,

00:52:57.126 --> 00:53:01.970
with increasing data set,
size, model size, and compute.

00:53:01.970 --> 00:53:05.910
and what align empirically has
been found in the past year

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:08.370
that most of the times
they follow power laws.

00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:12.860
But the question is what
properties of algorithms and models

00:53:12.860 --> 00:53:17.410
make those power laws converge
faster to smaller error,

00:53:17.410 --> 00:53:19.250
which ones are better.

00:53:19.250 --> 00:53:21.800
There is a hypothesis being discussed,

00:53:21.800 --> 00:53:24.120
whether scaling neural net models

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:25.860
can maybe solve continual learning

00:53:25.860 --> 00:53:30.210
and just get rid of
forgetting program completely.

00:53:30.210 --> 00:53:32.150
There's some promising results,

00:53:32.150 --> 00:53:36.223
but in very limited
setting with a few tasks.

00:53:37.670 --> 00:53:41.400
Oh, as you expect, larger models
should have more capacity,

00:53:41.400 --> 00:53:44.620
and yes, eventually they
can incorporate both tasks,

00:53:44.620 --> 00:53:47.610
or even like 10 tasks like
mentioned in the talk.

00:53:47.610 --> 00:53:52.280
But the question is, is
there a limit to that?

00:53:52.280 --> 00:53:55.970
And it seems that besides
scaling your model

00:53:55.970 --> 00:53:57.800
and the data you train on,

00:53:57.800 --> 00:54:01.003
you should probably
consider another dimension,

00:54:01.902 --> 00:54:04.770
which can be a complexity of
continual learning environment,

00:54:04.770 --> 00:54:08.540
in a sense of how diverse the tasks are.

00:54:08.540 --> 00:54:13.040
And if you push that complexity
of environment further,

00:54:13.040 --> 00:54:17.040
I bet that there must be some trade-off

00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:20.690
between that complexity and
the capacity of your model

00:54:20.690 --> 00:54:22.033
and size of your model.

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:26.200
And it would be interesting
to know what the trade off is.

00:54:27.513 --> 00:54:30.700
So you may need to scale
model beyond certain level

00:54:31.726 --> 00:54:34.250
to capture certain complexity
of the environment.

00:54:34.250 --> 00:54:39.120
And totally a speculative
slide is that perhaps

00:54:39.120 --> 00:54:44.120
to truly capture continual
learning the way humans do it,

00:54:44.777 --> 00:54:46.480
we may need to even go beyond

00:54:47.612 --> 00:54:51.650
the modern artificial neural
network kind of designs,

00:54:51.650 --> 00:54:53.100
and not just to allow

00:54:54.128 --> 00:54:55.460
for growing neural networks standard way,

00:54:55.460 --> 00:54:57.723
but maybe new types of neural networks,

00:54:58.815 --> 00:55:03.253
may be richer networks
allowing for extra capacity,

00:55:04.600 --> 00:55:05.684
for example,

00:55:05.684 --> 00:55:08.030
the representing time perhaps or not,

00:55:08.030 --> 00:55:09.870
that's an open question.

00:55:09.870 --> 00:55:14.870
So I will conclude here
by listing the ultimate

00:55:16.340 --> 00:55:20.040
wishlist of properties of
continual learning systems.

00:55:20.040 --> 00:55:24.030
And I also refer you to
various recent surveys,

00:55:24.030 --> 00:55:26.750
as I mentioned on the topic,

00:55:26.750 --> 00:55:29.713
and I'm pretty sure more surveys
being written as we speak.

00:55:31.729 --> 00:55:35.190
And thank you very much for more details.

00:55:35.190 --> 00:55:38.670
You can also take a look
at our recent ICML tutorial

00:55:38.670 --> 00:55:40.460
on the same subject.

00:55:40.460 --> 00:55:41.540
Thank you-

00:55:41.540 --> 00:55:42.530
- Amazing Irina,

00:55:42.530 --> 00:55:46.110
thank you so much for
just this just extensive

00:55:46.110 --> 00:55:48.660
and thorough survey of continual learning.

00:55:48.660 --> 00:55:51.480
I learned so much, I'm a
neuroscientist by training.

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:56.020
And so I was really
inspired by what you shared,

00:55:56.020 --> 00:55:58.710
how the insights from
neuroscience are being applied

00:55:58.710 --> 00:55:59.543
to this field and

00:55:59.543 --> 00:56:01.360
the state of the art
and the open questions,

00:56:01.360 --> 00:56:04.480
I think this is really, really
exciting area of research.

00:56:04.480 --> 00:56:07.460
We have a ton of questions
that have come in.

00:56:07.460 --> 00:56:09.580
I do remind our participants

00:56:09.580 --> 00:56:12.490
to upvote rather than
