00:56:15.670 --> 00:56:17.740
So we'll share these with
her after the lecture,

00:56:17.740 --> 00:56:21.060
and we'll even try to stay

00:56:21.060 --> 00:56:24.240
maybe for a total of five
minutes for the Q&amp;A right now.

00:56:24.240 --> 00:56:26.430
Irina, and so let's try to do

00:56:26.430 --> 00:56:29.560
some of the most popular
questions right off the bat.

00:56:29.560 --> 00:56:33.610
And so the first question is
to ask you to share with us

00:56:34.472 --> 00:56:38.243
a few examples of unsupervised
continual learning.

00:56:40.030 --> 00:56:43.760
- Well, one example is
Karol paper a few years ago,

00:56:43.760 --> 00:56:45.780
basically on stochastic learning.

00:56:45.780 --> 00:56:49.310
But you can think about in
a sense that it's nothing...

00:56:50.470 --> 00:56:52.736
There are several references,

00:56:52.736 --> 00:56:54.420
I'll be happy to provide
the list of papers,

00:56:54.420 --> 00:56:59.420
but in a sense, if you
think about trying to learn,

00:56:59.760 --> 00:57:04.070
say distribution of the
data that incorporates

00:57:04.070 --> 00:57:07.010
multiple different sub
distributions, roughly speaking,

00:57:07.010 --> 00:57:12.010
there is nothing too specific
to their supervised setting.

00:57:12.380 --> 00:57:13.686
I mean,

00:57:13.686 --> 00:57:15.860
you can think about
trying to capture variety

00:57:15.860 --> 00:57:18.100
of data distributions in one model,

00:57:18.100 --> 00:57:20.720
and those data distributions
will be responding to

00:57:20.720 --> 00:57:22.770
unsupervised setting where you would like

00:57:25.033 --> 00:57:26.746
to model your samples and be able

00:57:26.746 --> 00:57:27.673
to reconstruct them accurately.

00:57:28.870 --> 00:57:32.184
Yeah. So in a sense, it's not
surprising that there is this,

00:57:32.184 --> 00:57:34.750
the field is just a bit less explored

00:57:34.750 --> 00:57:37.520
just because of the of
supervised learning.

00:57:37.520 --> 00:57:42.520
But, yeah, I'd be happy to
provide more references.

00:57:42.890 --> 00:57:46.886
The paper Karol is like one
example that come to mind-

00:57:46.886 --> 00:57:50.290
- Sure. Yeah, we can just append
those to your Q&amp;A document,

00:57:50.290 --> 00:57:51.123
that's fine-

