really helpful resource.

00:58:08.340 --> 00:58:10.377
Okay, the next question is,

00:58:10.377 --> 00:58:12.350
"in a supervised setting,

00:58:12.350 --> 00:58:16.380
how can the imbalance in
the number of data samples

00:58:16.380 --> 00:58:18.847
per task impact the learning?"

00:58:24.081 --> 00:58:27.577
- Class imbalance is
always a potential issue.

00:58:28.910 --> 00:58:32.730
I think, I mean, there is no specific

00:58:32.730 --> 00:58:35.880
continual learning related consideration

00:58:37.754 --> 00:58:39.420
that kind of really just like

00:58:41.732 --> 00:58:43.362
with usual supervised learning,

00:58:43.362 --> 00:58:44.563
if you have high class imbalance,

00:58:45.949 --> 00:58:48.550
you might not be able
to represent properly

00:58:48.550 --> 00:58:49.420
as a second class,

00:58:49.420 --> 00:58:51.923
so you will need to do
something about that.

00:58:53.694 --> 00:58:57.065
And yeah, so I don't think
that continual learning adds

00:58:57.065 --> 00:58:57.898
any extra complexity.

00:58:57.898 --> 00:59:02.135
So any method that you would
use to handle class imbalance,

00:59:02.135 --> 00:59:03.976
perhaps can be incorporated

00:59:03.976 --> 00:59:05.411
in the continual learning setting.

00:59:05.411 --> 00:59:06.244
- Great. Okay.

00:59:06.244 --> 00:59:07.340
That's great advice.

00:59:07.340 --> 00:59:08.517
Next question,

00:59:08.517 --> 00:59:11.140
"How much does the curriculum matter

00:59:11.140 --> 00:59:13.150
in a continual learning setup?

00:59:13.150 --> 00:59:14.390
To design a curriculum,

00:59:14.390 --> 00:59:17.990
how would we go about creating
a suitable set of scaffolds

00:59:17.990 --> 00:59:19.790
to learn effectively?"

00:59:19.790 --> 00:59:24.017
- Yeah. This is a great question
and yes, it matters a lot.

00:59:24.017 --> 00:59:29.017
And indeed, I mean, especially with, yeah,

00:59:29.100 --> 00:59:32.390
with sufficiently different
distributions of data

00:59:32.390 --> 00:59:35.693
in different tasks,
again, the recitations,

00:59:36.596 --> 00:59:39.870
when gradual kind of
increase of complexity

00:59:39.870 --> 00:59:43.170
by slowly changing the task distribution

00:59:43.170 --> 00:59:45.593
may be better for model learn,

00:59:46.898 --> 00:59:48.201
instead of the drastic change.

00:59:48.201 --> 00:59:49.790
But it also depends on your approach

00:59:51.078 --> 00:59:52.363
and service drastic changes.

00:59:52.363 --> 00:59:56.050
Certain methods such
as maybe architectural

00:59:56.050 --> 00:59:59.370
that allow for expanding the architecture

01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:03.850
who are good at detecting
the changes drastic

01:00:03.850 --> 01:00:08.400
and introducing, and constructing
a new part of the model.

01:00:08.400 --> 01:00:13.010
Those might work better
if actually you designed

01:00:13.010 --> 01:00:14.950
the curriculum to be more challenging

01:00:16.503 --> 01:00:18.672
and the changes are more
abrupt, but in general,

01:00:18.672 --> 01:00:20.050
yes, it's highly relevant question.

01:00:20.050 --> 01:00:21.930
It's often empirical.

01:00:21.930 --> 01:00:24.130
The answers are frequently empirical.

01:00:24.130 --> 01:00:27.340
You need to investigate
for which type of methods

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:32.080
and models, what type of
curricula might be best.

01:00:32.080 --> 01:00:33.910
but in general, in general,

01:00:33.910 --> 01:00:35.540
the rule of thumb is that curriculum

01:00:35.540 --> 01:00:37.890
should be sufficiently challenging.

01:00:37.890 --> 01:00:42.723
If you would like to develop a
model that is more versatile.

01:00:44.062 --> 01:00:47.270
And definitely if you use
some very basic benchmarks

01:00:47.270 --> 01:00:50.760
such as rotated or permuted the MNIST,

01:00:50.760 --> 01:00:52.700
this is not enough of curriculum

01:00:52.700 --> 01:00:54.893
to learn truly generalizing models.

01:00:56.924 --> 01:00:58.110
- It has to be more complex than that.

01:00:58.110 --> 01:00:59.686
It's great.

01:00:59.686 --> 01:01:02.597
Okay, so last question that we
have time for to answer live,

01:01:02.597 --> 01:01:06.250
"How does continual learning
relate to the online learning

01:01:06.250 --> 01:01:07.720
literature?"

01:01:07.720 --> 01:01:09.620
- Yeah, I mean, it relates directly.

01:01:09.620 --> 01:01:12.400
So you essentially can think
about continual learning as

01:01:12.400 --> 01:01:15.810
online non-stationary learning.

01:01:15.810 --> 01:01:17.010
It is online learning,

01:01:17.010 --> 01:01:19.710
but the classical online
learning typically assumes

01:01:19.710 --> 01:01:24.100
that the data stream comes
from the same distribution,

01:01:24.100 --> 01:01:25.610
while continuing learning

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:28.450
assumes that there will be change points,

01:01:28.450 --> 01:01:31.380
whether abrupt or some kind of drifts.

01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:33.450
And that's the main difference between

01:01:33.450 --> 01:01:35.140
classical online learning

01:01:35.140 --> 01:01:39.110
and non-stationary or
continual online learning.

01:01:39.110 --> 01:01:41.237
- It's a lot more dynamic in
the continual learning context,

01:01:41.237 --> 01:01:42.290
right. Great.

01:01:42.290 --> 01:01:43.961
Okay. So I'm afraid.

01:01:43.961 --> 01:01:44.794
That's all we have time for, but Irina,

01:01:44.794 --> 01:01:46.715
what a wonderful lecture.

01:01:46.715 --> 01:01:49.228
Thank you so much for such a deep dive,

01:01:49.228 --> 01:01:50.310
such a thorough examination of the topic.

